  "notes": "Not a dwelling but the other building a town is made of, and the only one in the corpus. Every door is argued for rather than drawn. The bar is the whole plan: 'It must present itself at once to the eye of the customers on entering the house, for all enquiries are addressed here; and it must command, on all sides, by means of openings, or sliding sashes, the external door; so that it may not be possible for any person to enter or leave the house without observation.' The tap room is kept apart - 'it should not be possible for any person to enter or leave the Tap room without passing within view of the bar' - and the kitchen offices are cut off by a door by the staircase. Two yards, private and public, with a door between them so nobody has to go round the house. The tap room is optional: it 'may be omitted and its place occupied with another Parlor, or it may be placed in the yard'. 2800 pounds, or 3000 with the bar fittings.",
